#!/bin/sh
# mag art dry run for 004: 20 brief(s) x 2 variant(s).
# Run from the repo root. Every command renders one candidate into this
# round directory; rerunning a line overwrites only its own file.
set -eu

# cover-synthetic [cover]
imagegen 'A square abstract editorial cover illustration, at least 1000 by 1000 pixels, for a print magazine issue about the machinery that surrounds a machine-learning model: protocols, evaluations, architectures and human judgment that make a capable model dependable. Compose one compact abstract proposition from three to seven large geometric components: a single small solid element held, guided and bounded by larger structural forms around it, so the small element reads as subordinate to the system that carries it. Leave a substantial field of quiet negative space. Publication ink set only: warm off-white paper ground, charcoal ink, deep cobalt, brick coral, and one restrained muted-green or dusty-lavender accent, all at low to medium saturation with slight pigment variation, subtle paper tooth and quiet print grain. Controlled dimensional shading and synthetic material depth are welcome where they strengthen the proposition. Preserve a legible silhouette at thumbnail size and keep important detail away from the extreme edges. Judge anatomy of forms, materials, lighting, intersections and physical relationships as strictly as composition. Avoid all typography: no masthead, title, issue number, caption, logo, watermark, pseudo-writing, lettering or numerals anywhere in the image. Avoid literal diagrams, icon systems, circuit-board motifs, brain or robot imagery, generic-AI futurism, neon gradients, ornamental complexity and photorealism. — variation 1' --out editions/004/art/rounds/2026-08-06T22-59-03/cover-synthetic-v1.png
imagegen 'A square abstract editorial cover illustration, at least 1000 by 1000 pixels, for a print magazine issue about the machinery that surrounds a machine-learning model: protocols, evaluations, architectures and human judgment that make a capable model dependable. Compose one compact abstract proposition from three to seven large geometric components: a single small solid element held, guided and bounded by larger structural forms around it, so the small element reads as subordinate to the system that carries it. Leave a substantial field of quiet negative space. Publication ink set only: warm off-white paper ground, charcoal ink, deep cobalt, brick coral, and one restrained muted-green or dusty-lavender accent, all at low to medium saturation with slight pigment variation, subtle paper tooth and quiet print grain. Controlled dimensional shading and synthetic material depth are welcome where they strengthen the proposition. Preserve a legible silhouette at thumbnail size and keep important detail away from the extreme edges. Judge anatomy of forms, materials, lighting, intersections and physical relationships as strictly as composition. Avoid all typography: no masthead, title, issue number, caption, logo, watermark, pseudo-writing, lettering or numerals anywhere in the image. Avoid literal diagrams, icon systems, circuit-board motifs, brain or robot imagery, generic-AI futurism, neon gradients, ornamental complexity and photorealism. — variation 2' --out editions/004/art/rounds/2026-08-06T22-59-03/cover-synthetic-v2.png

# cover-art-directed [cover]
imagegen 'A square studio photograph, at least 1000 by 1000 pixels, made as an edition-specific artwork for a print magazine issue about how a small capable component only becomes dependable inside the larger system built around it. Photograph one physical constructed object on a warm off-white seamless background: a single small hand-thrown ceramic bead, glazed brick coral, held precisely at the centre of a much larger machined jig of turned wood and matte steel, with cobalt-anodised clamps and one muted-green calibration ring. The jig is honest workshop hardware, obviously made by hand, cradling rather than crushing the bead. Use soft directional daylight from the upper left, gentle falloff, true contact shadows, and materially convincing surfaces: glaze reflections, brushed metal, grain, faint dust. Colour stays in the publication palette at low to medium saturation: warm off-white, charcoal, deep cobalt, brick coral, muted green, small warm-ochre accents. Keep a strong readable silhouette at thumbnail size and keep important detail away from the extreme edges; print-friendly midtones, no crushed blacks. Do not include any typography, masthead, caption, logo, label, watermark, pseudo-writing or numerals. Do not include humans or hands. Do not include string, knots, paper scraps, diagrams, pinboards or collage elements. Avoid generic-AI futurism, glossy product-render sheen, neon lighting, impossible reflections and incoherent joinery. — variation 1' --out editions/004/art/rounds/2026-08-06T22-59-03/cover-art-directed-v1.png
