# cover-wildcard [cover]
imagegen 'A square, at least 1000 by 1000 pixels, single surprising image for the cover of a print magazine issue about capability held safely inside a system. One improbable proposition, immediately legible at thumbnail size: an enormous industrial bench vice of charcoal cast iron and cobalt-painted steel, jaws closed on a single perfect soap bubble that has not burst, the bubble iridescing faintly in brick coral, muted green and dusty lavender against a warm off-white studio void. Scale shock and arrested motion carry the image: the vice is monumental, the bubble is small, intact, and impossibly calm; the jaw faces dimple the film without breaking it. Photographic realism of materials, with believable contact between rubbered jaw faces and the bubble skin, true soft shadow beneath, and specular highlights that obey one light source from the upper left. Keep the silhouette unmistakable and centred, with clean margins and no important detail at the extreme edges; print-safe tonal range with open midtones and no muddy darks. No typography of any kind: no masthead, title, caption, logo, label, watermark, pseudo-writing or numerals. No humans, no hands. Avoid clutter, arbitrary symbols, floating debris, sparkles, neon cyberpunk, familiar AI-futurist imagery, incoherent physics and lens-flare spectacle. — variation 1' --out editions/004/art/rounds/2026-08-06T22-59-03/cover-wildcard-v1.png
